  "track": "track-01",
  "title": "C# pattern matching user-defined operators and conversions",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "Pattern matching tests a value's shape and can extract data while expressing branches. Patterns use language-defined rules and are distinct from ordinary operator overload resolution.",
    "A conversion changes how a value is viewed or transformed. Standard and user-defined conversion candidates interact with static typing and overload resolution; a conversion should clarify domain meaning without hiding expensive or lossy behavior.",
    "User-defined operators let value-like types participate in familiar expressions, but overloads should preserve expected mathematical or logical semantics. Named methods are clearer when an operation is asymmetric, expensive, or surprising.",
    "Switch expressions combine patterns and optional guards into a result-producing decision. Exhaustiveness depends on the input type and compiler knowledge of its alternatives; a discard arm is useful when alternatives may grow.",
    "Use relational and logical patterns for bounded decisions, and property, positional, or list patterns to match structure. Keep guards side-effect free so branch selection remains easy to reason about."
  ],
